# Concept

A `Concept` is the fundamental semantic unit of HQL.

It is an abstract, identifiable thing that can participate in [[knowledge]].

A concept may be referred to, described, related to other concepts, claimed about, supported by evidence, contradicted, inferred, constrained, certified, or proved.

```text
Concept : Type
```

A concept is not merely a node in a graph and it is not necessarily a document.

## Concept and Card

A [[Card]] is an authored HMD document.

A `Concept` is the abstract thing that a card may describe.

```text
Card != Concept
```

For example:

```hql
aliceCard : Card = resolve([[alice]])
```

may describe the concept:

```text
Alice : Person
```

Conceptually:

```text
aliceCard --describes--> Alice
```

The distinction is important because the same concept may be represented by many different artifacts:

```text
Alice
├── alice.hmd
├── external document
├── database record
├── photograph
├── claims about Alice
└── references from other cards
```

None of these representations is identical to the concept itself.

A concept may also exist without having a dedicated card.

## Concepts have identity

A concept must be addressable independently of any particular representation.

Two references may therefore resolve to the same concept even when they originate from different cards or external sources.

Conceptual identity answers:

> What thing are these statements about?

rather than:

> Which document contains the text?

This gives two separate identities:

```text
Card identity
Concept identity
```

They may often correspond one-to-one, but HQL should not require that.

## Concepts remain typed

`Concept` is a common semantic root, not an untyped container.

Specific concepts retain more precise types.

For example:

```text
Alice       : Person
Fatherhood  : Relation<Person, Person>
Claim #42   : Claim
Proof #7    : Proof
```

Conceptually:

```text
Person      <: Concept
Relation    <: Concept
Claim       <: Concept
Evidence    <: Concept
Proof       <: Concept
Constraint  <: Concept
```

The exact type-system mechanism is still open. HQL may eventually express this through subtyping, traits, type classes, dependent types, or another mechanism.

The important rule is:

```text
conceptual identity != loss of static type
```

Everything may participate in the same knowledge universe while still preserving its precise type.

## Relations are concepts

A relation can itself be a concept.

For example:

```text
fatherOf : Relation<Person, Person>
```

`fatherOf` is the abstract relation.

Applying it to concrete concepts produces a proposition:

```text
fatherOf(john, alice)
```

So these are different:

```text
fatherOf
```

the relation concept,

and:

```text
fatherOf(john, alice)
```

a proposition involving concepts.

This distinction prevents HQL from reducing knowledge to anonymous graph edges.

## Claims are concepts

A claim is also something that may need identity.

Suppose John's card declares:

```hql#declare
relatedTo -> [[alice]] { kind = father }
```

This does not directly establish:

```text
fatherOf(john, alice)
```

as truth.

It introduces a claim equivalent to:

```text
John claims that fatherOf(john, alice)
```

That claim can itself participate in knowledge:

```text
Claim #42
├── declared by John's card
├── supported by Evidence #3
├── contradicted by Claim #81
└── evaluated by Assessment #6
```

Making claims first-class concepts allows HQL to reason not only about people and things, but also about assertions concerning them.

## Evidence, proofs, and constraints can be concepts

The same principle extends to other epistemic objects.

For example:

```text
Birth Certificate #17 : Evidence
Proof #12              : Proof
Constraint #4          : Constraint
Assessment #8          : Assessment
```

These objects may themselves be referenced and related.

```text
Evidence #17
    --supports-->
Claim #42
```

```text
Proof #12
    --proves-->
Claim #91
```

```text
Constraint #4
    --conflicts-with-->
Claim #42
```

This makes the epistemic structure itself part of the knowledge model.

## Not every temporary value is necessarily a concept

HQL may evaluate values that do not need permanent conceptual identity.

For example:

```text
42
true
"alice"
fatherOf(john, alice)
```

may exist temporarily during evaluation.

A value becomes a concept when it is epistemically addressable: when HQL needs to identify it, reference it, describe it, attach provenance to it, relate other concepts to it, or reason about it as an object.

Therefore:

```text
Value != Concept
```

and:

```text
Concept != every runtime value
```

`Concept` belongs to the semantic knowledge model, not merely to the evaluator's object model.

## Concept and knowledge

The [[A Universe of Knowledge|knowledge universe]] is formed from concepts and the typed relationships between them.

Conceptually:

```text
knowledge : Universe<Concept>
```

A concept is therefore the atomic addressable unit of that universe.

Examples include:

```text
a person
a place
an idea
a theorem
a relation
a claim
a document
a piece of evidence
a proof
a constraint
an assessment
```

What makes them concepts is not that they share the same data structure.

What they share is that HQL can treat them as identifiable participants in knowledge.

## Definition

> A `Concept` is an abstract, identifiable, epistemically addressable thing that can participate in the HQL knowledge universe while retaining its own precise type.

Or more compactly:

```text
Concept = something knowledge can be about
```
